al  algorithms at small k  can't see the whole graph it's a  restricted model of computation  and so  the question here is can local  algorithms perform well  on these problems  they should  uh be limited in some capacity  also  local algorithms aren't just  you know limited to classical algorithms  i'm also interested in quantum  computation  and  the the state of quantum computers at  this time is that we have these nisk  machines and kind of noisy machines  where you can run a few  algorithms and  but you can't run very much every  operation which is like a two-wire  operation  um  is  was adds contributes some noise to the  system and so you can only do so many of  these before you  let noise overwhelms the signal  and so we're modeling this kind of  computation with local quantum  algorithms and in fact for combinatorial  optimization the most popular  uh  quantum algorithm to run in the near  term is the qaoa or  which is an example of a local quantum  algorithm  but the point here is that with these  noisy machines you can't have a very  high circuit depth  and so your computation is restricted  and so the big question the overarching  question here is are these near-term  machines useful for combinatorial  optimization compared to regular  computers  graphic that might  sum up some of this is you have these q  local machines and q local at depth one  is this green line  q local at some constant depth is the  yellow line  and the classical algorithms are the  black line  and the question is you know do the does  the green or the yellow line you know  cross  the black line  and if there's some crossing then there  is some  you know csp where you can solve it  better  on a local quantum  using a local quantum algorithm which  you could theoretically use a quantum  computer in this decade  to solve  but we're not even sure if this happens  so we've named this nisk optimization  advantage we're not sure if this is  possible there's no theory to support it  yet  and we have a couple results maybe  suggesting it might not be possible  there's this blue line i've written here  to the scalable fault tolerant quantum  computers so the quantum computers of  the long term after you have error  correction and low noise  and  you know millions of qubits what can you  do and you can do anything a classical  computer does and you might actually be  able to do more but again we're not sure  about any of that and it won't be  relevant for many years  so what's the state of the art for  maximum cut  so  here i have a line plot and the line  plot is cutting on random graphs in a  random graph again you have half plus  some constant over square root d  that's the cut fraction or the the  percent of edges that you can actually  cut on a random graph  and so  through some a really fascinating  connection to spin glass theory the  pre-c value 0.76 something  is the optimum you can do on a random  graph as n and d grow large and so that  0.76 on the right side is kind of your  your  target  now when the qa was analyzed at its  lowest step this qaoa is the  popular quantum algorithm that is also  local  and at the lowest depth they got to 0.31  and  it's close to 0.31 it's like 1 over 2  square root e or something  but the point is that it was actually  the best known algorithm at least  measured on this metric  and so a big question was wait is the  qaa really successful on this problem  and it turns out in 2019 the answer is  no  and there was a re-analysis of a of a  threshold algorithm that was classical  and again local  and to show that this one local  algorithm also is  gets about 0.33 which is a little better  than the qaoa but the point is the qaa  is not you know special here  and of course you know this is just at  the lowest depth  what about a higher depth so i did some  work last year that  said no it's actually not true at two  either you know the qaoa does better but  so does this threshold algorithm  and so  a question here is like how well does a  quantum algorithm do but also how well  do classical algorithms do  and so we have two results in this in  this  document  the first result is that all one local  algorithms are bounded away from optimal  and so this is using a really cool new  technique of random walks of  correlations  um  this shows that any one local algorithm  quantum or classical  cannot achieve higher than this 0.71  value so it's a non-trivial result it's  kind of weak  and there's probably lots of ways it  could be  extended but it does show a block like  an um  a maximum that you can achieve using one  local algorithm  we also have a result that i won't talk  about in this talk that is a  a polytime classical algorithm that gets  to 0.63  and this is a restatement of some of  some earlier ideas on gaussian waves  and  it's uh has some relationship to the  semi-definite uh programming algorithm  uh but it's actually outdated you know  it doesn't  it doesn't  it's not super important anymore because  as of november  uh  andrea montanari and and collaborators  found  a classical algorithm that's  asymptotically optimal  so actually there is a gap between these  one local algorithms quantum or  classical and the classical algorithm so  there's a classical algorithm that gets  optimal  at least asymptotically  and  this shows that at least for max cut on  random graphs that yellow line in that  figure will never cross the black line  okay so let's i'll give a brief overview  of the main technique which i think is  it's pretty cool  so  the theorem we have is that when the  girth of a graph is at least six  a one local algorithm can output a cut  of expected value half plus c over  square root d  where c is less than some value and it's  1 over square root 2 about 0.71  okay so how do we get this  so  i mentioned before that i'm using  correlations of random blocks  so if you have a random walk on a graph  after you walk let's say s steps  you're going to be some distance away  from the origin and that distance  you know it's probabilistic and  you can get there you know you can  calculate the chance of being distance d  from the origin  so if your distance one away from the  origin  the correlation of labels that are  adjacent  is actually related to the cut the  chance of being cut  because if your labels are different  then you'll be in different partitions  and the edge will be cut  if your labels are the same you'll be in  the same partition so you won't be cut  so the distance one correlation is  actually  very  it has a  really  simple connection to the cut fraction  and  if you have a correlation at some  distance that's large over 2k  um for a k local algorithm then you'll  be outside of the light cone so to speak  of this k local algorithm and so there  can't be any correlation by the  definition of a local algorithm  and this definition of local algorithm  has really nothing to do with classical  or quantum just the idea of locality  and so any results we prove using these  methods  are going to be  true for quantum and for classical  algorithms  so first we relate like uh you know  we're going to look at one local  algorithms and so three-step walks if  you you know let's look at a three-step  random walk  if you walk three random steps you're  either gonna be distance one or distance  three away  and if your distance one away it's like  you took a one-step random walk but if  your distance three-way there's no  correlation for at least for one local  algorithm you're outside of the light  cone  and so you can relate the you know the  chance of correlation  uh from a three-step random walk to the  chance of correlation from a one-step  random walk because in both  examples there's only one contribution  from distance one away  so this is kind of like the key point  here that you can relate  both  you know walks that travel too far and  you have zero correlation and walks that  travel really close  and distance one means that's the kind  of the  thing we're looking for  so from here we use koshi swartz and we  can say that when you look when you  random walk let's say  four times  it's as if you apply the adjacency  matrix four times  because you're randomly going to a  neighbor which you know does involve  backtracking  and so because you know in this form you  can you can use a cushy schwartz to say  the chance of walking four steps  is going to be at least as big as  you know  you know the correlation at four steps  is going to be at least as big as  walking two steps squared or walking one  step to the fourth  and you can use this inequality to  relate you know two-step to four-step  random walks to one-step random walks  and so putting all this together you're  able to bound  the expected correlation of a one-step  random walk which is just a distance one  you know that's just looking at  neighbors  which  the cut fraction is exactly related to  this value this two over square root d  becomes you know the c becomes one over  square root  two so hopefully this was an overview of  how you can use correlations of random  walks to bound things about local  algorithms  because when you're very close  that's the value you're seeking and when  you're very far you know the correlation  is zero  unfortunately we weren't able to  generalize this  to higher locality with you know and  still get non-trivial bounds but it's  possible that related problems we can  use this technique and  still find out something new about local  algorithms  okay so what's next so so on max cut you  know the overarching question that at  least i've been after is do classical  algorithms outperform all quantum local  algorithms and for max cut on random  graphs asymptotically the answer is yes  by the montsonari paper  but we don't know let's say on graphs of  large but finite girth  or let's say a constant depth local  algorithm that's classical versus a  constant depth quantum algorithm  that's local also  and we also you know there's kind of a  bigger question here is is  quantum computing useful for  combinatorial optimization and i think  there's some real theoretical doubts  that there's no valid like there's no  proven  area  that shows that quantum computing even  in its fault tolerant way  is better for combinatorial optimization  than classical computing  and so not only is this may not be  useful in the near term this may not be  of a great direction for quantum  computers to look at  and so if we're spending a lot of money  thinking about quantum computing for  optimization maybe it's best to put that  money somewhere else  so with that thanks uh i'm at uchicago  now and you can visit my website  um one other thing i wanted to mention  is we put a summary of this work on the  archive wiki which is like a place you  can put summaries and notes of the  papers you read you can explore by topic  you can look at a bunch of papers about  the qaoa  i'm looking for contributors i  started the site last year  feel free to email me about it  and thanks

